WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.235
"Perceptions of Childhood Obesity
and Diet Quality"

00:00:06.582 --> 00:00:09.920
In response to the rising rates
of childhood obesity,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.760
English children started
getting screened at school

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.320
and their parents informed
of their kid's weight status

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.280
and the risk
of excess body fat.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.080
It did not go well.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.080
Telling parents that
a lifetime of excess body fat

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.160
can increase cancer risk,
for example,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.040
caused "controversy and anger."

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.800
Parents attributed weight
to genetics or puppy fat,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.440
claiming their kids
had healthy diets.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.240
One reason kids may not be
eating more healthfully

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.920
is that their parents
overestimate the quality

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.600
of their child's diet.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.840
If it ain't broke,
don't fix it.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.520
I mean, if they think their kids
are already eating well,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.080
then there's
no reason to change.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.920
The mothers of 2,000
preschoolers were interviewed.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.760
What percentage of moms believed

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.120
that their child's diet
was good?

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.680
The vast majority.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.080
What percentage of their kids'
diets were actually good,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.600
even just according to the U.S.
Department of Agriculture's

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.680
Healthy Eating guidelines,
which aren't exactly strict?

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.160
Only 0.2 percent--
1 in every 500 kids!

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.080
The vast majority of mothers
overestimated the quality

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.360
of their child's diet.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.920
Here's an updated study.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.760
Another 2,000 households,

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.320
just seeing if kids meet
the fruit, vegetable,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.840
and sugar recommendations from
the U.S. dietary guidelines

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.040
and the American
Heart Association.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.440
Researchers looked at kids
from ages 3 through 18

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.600
and relied on reporting
from their own parents

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.480
as to what they were eating.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.560
Overall, the majority of parents
strongly agreed

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.680
that their kids
ate healthfully.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.760
So, how many of the 2,229
households met the guidelines

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.280
to eat even the minimum
recommended amounts

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.200
of fruits and veggies

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.280
and not eat more than
six spoonfuls of sugar a day?

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.560
Zero.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.920
Not a single kid
in a single household.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.760
On average, the kids were
eating more than 15 spoonfuls

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.320
of added sugar every day.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.960
Parental denial
over the healthfulness

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.880
of their children's diet
mirrors the parental denial

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.000
that their overweight and obese
kids are even overweight at all.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.320
Based on surveys
in more than 20 countries,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.280
94 percent of parents
with obese children

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.520
believe that their children
are normal weight

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.400
or only "a little overweight."

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.240
94 percent!

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.280
And the greater
the obesity prevalence,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.880
the more likely parents think
their kids look normal,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.360
because they kind of do,
more and more.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.440
But this underestimation
of their child's weight status

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.760
is a barrier to slowing
the childhood obesity epidemic.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.600
And just as parents
of overweight kids

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.040
fail to recognize
that their kids are overweight,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.080
overweight parents are also in
denial about their own weight.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.360
This is not surprising.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:56.840
Who wants to admit they are part
of a widely stigmatized group?

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.400
Of course, researchers
aren't helping matters

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.560
when they title their papers
about parental misperception

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.400
about overweight kids:

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.212
"Every cockroach is beautiful
to his mother's eyes."

